Ladybird centers script and sequential keyboard focus independently
at each ancestor scroll container. An axis retains its scroll offset
when the target’s border box is fully visible or covers the whole
scrollport. Other axes use centered alignment, with scroll-margin and
scroll-padding applied after the visibility check. Click focus,
preventScroll, autofocus, and ordinary scrollIntoView() retain their
separate scrolling behavior. The accessibility focus action requests
the same Key trigger as sequential navigation, so it also centers
offscreen focusable elements when needed.

Blink routes sequential navigation through FocusController::
AdvanceFocusInDocumentOrder() into Element::Focus(). The focus path
uses Element::UpdateSelectionOnFocus() and the default arguments of
scroll_into_view_util::CreateScrollIntoViewParams(), which select
ScrollAlignment::CenterIfNeeded(). ScrollRectToVisible() walks the
ancestors, and CalculateScrollAlignment() treats a target covering
the scrollport as visible, preserving that axis instead of centering.

Gecko’s nsFocusManager::ScrollIntoView() selects WhereToScroll::Center
and WhenToScroll::IfNotVisible on both axes. PresShell evaluates
ComputeNeedToScroll() before ComputeWhereToScroll(). Its visibility
condition leaves a covering target unchanged. The visibility test
also allows some partial visibility, so it differs from Ladybird for
partially visible ordinary elements. TextControlElement::
ScrollSelectionIntoViewAsync() also reveals text-control selections.

WebKit’s FocusController::advanceFocusInDocumentOrder() calls the
ordinary element focus path. Element::updateFocusAppearance() defers
scrolling to LocalFrameView::scrollToFocusedElementInternal(), which
uses ScrollAlignment::alignCenterIfNeeded on both axes. The helper
ScrollableArea::getRectToExposeForScrollIntoView() preserves axes
where the target fits inside, or covers, the entire visible interval.

Runtime probes used Chrome 157.0.8103.78, Firefox 151.0, and Playwright
WebKit 26.5. All three agree on 14 keyboard cases: visible and offscreen
targets, each axis separately, nested scrollers, oversized covering
targets, and oversized offscreen targets. All three also preserve
covered axes in the 11 oversized script-focus cases. Measurements
wait two animation frames because WebKit defers its focus scrolling.

HTML’s focus() steps require centered block and inline alignment.
Ladybird deliberately preserves fully visible and fully covered axes
for interoperability, and applies that alignment to keyboard focus.
For partially visible targets that don’t cover the scrollport,
Ladybird retains centered alignment. Chrome and WebKit reveal the
nearest edge of a partially visible ordinary element, while Firefox
leaves it unchanged. For a partially visible input, Firefox and WebKit
center it, while Chrome reveals its nearest edge. Autofocus still uses
FocusTrigger::Other, so this change doesn’t fix the issue’s autofocus
example. The engine sources were inspected for behavior only.
